## REACT ROUTER v7 INTEGRATION

```typescript
// In React Router v7, ErrorBoundary is a named export from route files:
// NOT wrapped with createBoundary() — just export directly

// app/routes/app.quizzes.tsx
export async function loader({ request }: LoaderFunctionArgs) {
  const { session } = await authenticate.admin(request);
  try {
    const quizzes = await getQuizzes(session.shop);
    return json({ quizzes });
  } catch (error) {
    if (error instanceof Response) throw error;  // Re-throw auth redirects
    console.error("Loader error:", error);
    throw json({ error: "Failed to load quizzes" }, { status: 500 });
  }
}

// ErrorBoundary catches the thrown json above:
export { QuizSnapErrorBoundary as ErrorBoundary };
```

---

## SELF-VALIDATION CHECKLIST

- [ ] Every `app.*.tsx` route exports `ErrorBoundary`
- [ ] 404 errors show helpful "not found" UI with navigation back
- [ ] 403 errors link to billing upgrade
- [ ] 500 errors show "something went wrong" with retry option
- [ ] JS errors show the error message (not a blank page)
- [ ] All error UIs use Polaris components (Page, Card, Button)
- [ ] Error boundaries use `backAction` so users can navigate out
- [ ] Shared `QuizSnapErrorBoundary` imported from `~/components/shared/`
- [ ] Storefront widget wraps in try/catch + unhandledrejection listener

---

## NEVER

- Never show a blank white page on errors (Law 9)
- Never expose raw stack traces to users in production
- Never use `window.alert()` for errors (use Banner + ErrorBoundary)
- Never forget to re-throw `Response` errors from loaders (auth redirects)
- Never create error boundaries without navigation out (always provide a CTA)
- Never let widget errors break the merchant's storefront page

